==== 2. Positively: What It Is

#smallcaps[Next] we must consider what it is to mortify a sin in general. This will prepare the way further for particular directions.

The mortification of a lust consists of three things:

(1.) It is an #emph[habitual] weakening of the lust. Every lust is a depraved habit or disposition that continually inclines the heart to evil. That is why the man who has no lust truly mortified is described this way: “every inclination of the thoughts of his heart was altogether evil all the time” (Gen 6:5). He is always under the power of a strong bent and inclination to sin. A natural man is not perpetually chasing some one lust night and day, and the reason is that he has many lusts to serve, every one crying out to be satisfied. So he is carried along in great variety, but in general he always leans toward satisfying self.

We will suppose, then, that the lust or disorder whose mortification we are asking about is in itself a strong, deeply rooted, habitual inclination and bent of the will and affections toward some act that is sinful in its matter, though not desired formally as sin; and it is always stirring up imaginations, thoughts, and schemes about its object. Hence men are said to have their “hearts set on evil”; the bent of their spirits lies toward it, to make “provision for the desires of the flesh.”#footnote[Rom 13:14.] And a sinful, depraved habit differs here, as in many other things, from all natural or moral habits whatever. Those incline the soul gently and in a way that suits it, but sinful habits drive it with violence and force. That is why lusts are said to fight or wage “war against your soul”#footnote[Στρατεύονται κατὰ τῆς ψυχῆς.] (1 Pet 2:11); to rebel or rise up in war, with the strategy and opposition usual in war#footnote[Ἀντιστρατευόμενον, αἰχμαλωτίζοντα.] (Rom 7:23); and to lead captive, that is, to take prisoners after winning the battle. All these are works of great violence and force.

I could show fully, from the description of it in Romans 7, how it darkens the mind, extinguishes convictions, dethrones reason, interrupts the power and influence of any considerations brought to restrain it, and breaks through everything into a flame. But that is not my business now. The first thing in mortification, then, is the weakening of this habit of sin or lust, so that it does not rise up, conceive, stir up tumult, provoke, entice, and disquiet with the violence, earnestness, and frequency it naturally tends to (Jas 1:14, 15).

I want to give one caution or rule by the way. It is this: every lust, by its own nature, inclines and drives to sin equally and universally; yet this must be granted with two limitations:

\[1.\] #emph[One lust], or a lust in one man, may receive many accidental boosts, heightenings, and reinforcements. These may give it life, power, and vigor far beyond what another lust has, or what the same lust (that is, of the same kind and nature) has in another man. A lust may fall in with a man’s natural constitution and temperament, with a suitable way of life, or with opportunities; or Satan may have got a fit handle on it to manage it, as he has a thousand ways of doing. Then that lust grows violent and forceful above others, or more than the same lust in another man. Then its fumes so darken the mind that, though a man knows the same things as before, they have no power or influence on his will; instead, corrupt affections and passions are set free by it.

But above all, lust gets strength by #emph[temptation]. When a suitable temptation falls in with a lust, it gives it new life, vigor, power, violence, and rage, which it did not seem to have before or to be capable of. Examples of this could be multiplied; but proving this observation is the aim of part of another treatise.

\[2.\] Some lusts are far more felt and noticeable in their violent workings than others. Paul puts a difference between sexual impurity and all other sins: “Flee from sexual immorality. Every other sin a man can commit is outside his body, but he who sins sexually sins against his own body” (1 Cor 6:18). So the motions of that sin are more felt and more noticeable than those of others. Yet perhaps the love of the world, or something like it, is no less habitually dominant in a person than that sin; it just does not cause so great a commotion in the whole man.

And for this reason some men may pass, in their own thoughts and in the eyes of the world, for mortified men. Yet lust is no less dominant in them than in those who cry out in amazement at its perplexing turmoil, yes, than in those whom its power has hurried into scandalous sins. The only difference is that their lusts are in and about things that do not raise such a tumult in the soul. They are occupied with them in a calmer frame of spirit, because the very fabric of their nature is not so closely concerned in them as in some others.

I say, then, that the first thing in mortification is the #emph[weakening] of this habit, so that it does not drive and stir up tumult as before, does not entice and draw aside, does not disquiet and perplex. It is the killing of its life, vigor, promptness, and readiness to be stirring. This is called having “crucified the flesh with its passions and desires” (Gal 5:24): that is, taking away its blood and spirits, which give it strength and power. It is the wasting away of the body of death “day by day” (2 Cor 4:16).

Think of a man #emph[nailed to the cross]. At first he struggles and strives and cries out with great strength and might. But as his blood and spirits drain away, his strivings grow faint and rare, his cries low and hoarse, scarcely to be heard. So it is when a man first sets on a lust or disorder to deal with it. It struggles with great violence to break loose; it cries out with earnestness and impatience to be satisfied and relieved. But when mortification lets out its blood and spirits, it moves rarely and faintly, cries out seldom, and is scarcely heard in the heart. Sometimes it may have a dying pang that gives an appearance of great vigor and strength, but this is quickly over, especially if it is kept from any considerable success. The apostle describes this throughout the whole chapter, and especially in Rom 6:6.

“Sin,” he says, “is crucified; it is fastened to the cross.” To what end? “So that the body of sin might be rendered powerless”: the power of sin weakened and abolished little by little, “that we should no longer be slaves to sin.” That is, so that sin might not incline and drive us with such force as to make us its servants, as it has done before. And this is said not only of carnal and sensual affections, or desires for worldly things (the lust of the flesh, the lust of the eyes, and the pride of life), but also of the flesh as it is in the mind and will, in that opposition to God which is in us by nature. Whatever the nature of the troubling disorder, whatever ways it shows itself, whether by driving to evil or by hindering from good, the rule is the same. Unless this is done effectively, all later struggling will not reach the end aimed at. A man may beat down the bitter fruit from an evil tree until he is weary; while the root remains in strength and vigor, beating down the present fruit will not stop it from bringing forth more. This is the folly of some men. They set themselves with all earnestness and diligence against the visible outbreaks of lust, but they leave the principle and root untouched, perhaps not even searched out, and so they make little or no progress in this work of mortification.

(2.) Mortification consists in constant #emph[fighting] and #emph[contending] against sin. To be able always to be laying heavy blows on sin is no small degree of mortification. When sin is strong and vigorous, the soul is scarcely able to make any headway against it. It sighs and groans and mourns and is troubled, as David speaks of himself, but it seldom pursues sin. David complains that his sins had “overtaken” him, so that he could not look up (Ps 40:12). How little, then, was he able to fight against it! Now, several things are required for this fighting against sin and are included in it:

\[1.\] It is required that a man #emph[know] he has such an enemy to deal with: that he take notice of it and consider it a real enemy, one to be destroyed by every possible means. As I said before, the contest is vigorous and dangerous; it is about the things of eternity. So when men have slight and passing thoughts of their lusts, it is no great sign that they are mortified, or that they are on the way to their mortification. This is every man’s “knowing the affliction of his own heart” (1 Kgs 8:38), without which no other work can be done. It is to be feared that very many have little knowledge of the chief enemy they carry about in their bosoms. This makes them ready to justify themselves and impatient of reproof or admonition, not knowing they are in any danger (2 Chr 16:10).

\[2.\] The beginning of this warfare is to labor to become acquainted with the ways, wiles, methods, advantages, and occasions of its #emph[success]. This is how men deal with enemies. They search out their counsels and plans, weigh their aims, and consider how and by what means they have prevailed before, so that they may be forestalled. The greatest skill in generalship consists in this. Take this away, and all waging of war, in which human wisdom and industry are most fully employed, would be brutish. So do those who truly mortify lust deal with it. Not only when it is actually troubling, enticing, and seducing them, but in their private hours, they consider: “This is our enemy. This is his way and progress; these are his advantages; this is how he has prevailed, and this is what he will do if he is not forestalled.” So David says, “My sin is always before me” (Ps 51:3). Indeed, one of the choicest and most excellent parts of practical spiritual wisdom consists in finding out the subtleties, policies, and depths of any indwelling sin. It is to consider and know where its greatest strength lies; what advantage it tends to make of occasions, opportunities, and temptations; what its pleas, pretenses, and reasonings are; what its stratagems, disguises, and excuses are. It is to set the wisdom of the Spirit against the craft of the #emph[old man]\; to trace this serpent in all its turnings and windings; to be able to say, at its most secret workings (imperceptible to an ordinary frame of heart), “This is your old way and course; I know what you are aiming at.” And so to be always in readiness is a good part of our warfare.

\[3.\] The height of this contest is to load it daily with all the things that will be mentioned later, which are grievous, killing, and destructive to it. Such a man never thinks his lust is dead because it is quiet, but still labors to give it new wounds and new blows every day. So does the apostle (Col 3:5).

Now, while the soul is in this condition, while it is dealing with sin like this, it is certainly uppermost; sin is under the sword and #emph[dying].

(3.) Mortification consists in #emph[success]. Frequent success against any lust is another part and evidence of mortification. By success I do not mean a mere thwarting of sin, so that it is not brought forth or accomplished. I mean a victory over it, and a pursuit of it to complete conquest. For instance, when the heart finds sin at work at any time, seducing and forming imaginations to make provision for the flesh, to fulfill its lusts, it instantly arrests sin, brings it to the law of God and the love of Christ, condemns it, and follows it with execution to the uttermost.

Now, I say, when a man comes to this state and condition, then sin is mortified in some considerable measure: when lust is weakened in its root and principle, so that its motions and actions are fewer and weaker than before, and cannot hinder his duty or interrupt his peace; when he can, in a quiet, calm frame of spirit, find out sin and fight against it and have success against it. And then, for all its opposition, a man may have peace with God all his days.

To these heads, then, I refer the mortification aimed at, that is, the mortification of any one perplexing disorder through which the general depravity and corruption of our nature tries to exert and put itself forth:

First, the foundation of it is the #emph[weakening] of its indwelling disposition, by which it inclines, entices, and drives to evil, rebels, opposes, and fights against God. This is done by the implanting, the settled dwelling, and the cherishing of a principle of grace that stands in direct opposition to it and destroys it. So pride is weakened by the implanting and growth of humility, passion by patience, impurity by purity of mind and conscience, love of this world by heavenly-mindedness. These are graces of the Spirit, or the same habitual grace acting in various ways by the Holy Spirit, according to the variety or diversity of the objects it is exercised about; just as the others are several lusts, or the same natural corruption acting in various ways, according to the various advantages and occasions it meets with.

A second thing required is the #emph[promptness, eagerness, vigor] of the Spirit, or the new man, in contending with and cheerfully fighting against the lust in question, by all the ways and with all the means appointed for it, constantly using the help provided against its motions and workings.

#emph[Several degrees] of success attend these two. If the disorder does not have an unconquerable advantage from its natural situation, this success may possibly reach such a #emph[universal] conquest that the soul never again sensibly feels its opposition. And in any case it will certainly rise to a granting of peace to the conscience, according to the terms of the covenant of grace.
